#
# RESOLVED (July 2026, named observation channels) — kept as a
# regression note.
#
# The problem was: one word family, four constructs, with quoting
# alone flipping a condition's semantic domain —
#
#   stop              verb: host-side hard power-off, an action
#   stopped           bare condition: backend reports not running
#   machine stopped   header: a precondition, performs nothing
#   "stopped"         quoted: guest SCREEN TEXT saying "stopped"
#
# A reader skimming a column of wait lines could not tell a
# machine-event wait from a text wait without focusing on the
# quotes:
#
#     wait "Formatting complete"      guest screen
#     wait stopped                    machine state   (OLD SURFACE)
#     wait "stopped"                  guest screen again
#
# THE FIX: the screen is the default channel and a bare string or
# regex IS a screen observation (its only spelling); every other
# channel is named as a prefix, and the bare `stopped` condition
# no longer exists:

description "The stop/stopped family — resolved by channels"
platform    dos
machine     stopped     # header: still an inert precondition
timeout     30s

start                   # verb: acts now
enter "shutdown"
wait  "stopped"         # a screen observation: guest text
wait  machine=stopped   # channel named: unmistakably machine state
# wait stopped          # ILLEGAL: no bare machine-state condition

# The domains can no longer swap on a quoting slip: the machine
# domain always carries its channel name, and a quoted word is
# always the screen. The fix also opened the growth path — image=
# and console= arrive as new channel names, not new syntax (see
# the spec's channel table and vocabulary-growth section).
#
# RESIDUE (minor, accepted): the word "stopped" still appears in
# two inert-vs-observed roles — `machine stopped` (header
# precondition, noun-first zone) and `machine=stopped` (condition
# value) — and `stop` the verb remains destructive beside both.
# The zone rule and the channel spelling now carry the
# distinction; no further fix planned unless real authoring
# confusion shows up.
